.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt IXLNVM 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ixlnvm
.Nd manage the NVM of Intel 700-series Ethernet controllers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm ixlnvm
.Op Cm scan Oo Fl q Oc Oo Fl c Ar nvmupdate.cfg Oc
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm dump
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm show
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm cfg-read Ar feature_id
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm selftest
.Op Fl y
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm image-diff Ar file
.Nm ixlnvm
.Cm image-show Ar file
.Nm ixlnvm
.Op Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm match
.Fl c Ar nvmupdate.cfg
.Op Fl e Ar eepid
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm verify-checksum
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm image-apply
.Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl y
.Nm ixlnvm
.Fl i Ar ifname
.Cm module-update
.Fl m Ar module
.Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl y
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads and updates the non-volatile memory
.Pq Dq NVM ,
also referred to as Flash, of an Intel 700-series Ethernet controller
attached via the
.Xr ixl 4
driver.
It uses the kernel's NVM session ioctls to hold ownership of the
NVM resource across multiple admin-queue commands.
.Pp
The interface to operate on is selected with
.Fl i Ar ifname .
.Pp
The available commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Op Cm scan Oo Fl q Oc Oo Fl c Ar nvmupdate.cfg Oc
.Xc
List every
.Xr ixl 4
interface with its NVM version, running firmware version and
EETRACK ID, grouping the ports of each physical card by PCI bus and
device.
This is the default if no command is given, and it does not take
.Fl i .
The
.Fl q
and
.Fl c
options require the
.Cm scan
command to be given explicitly.
.Pp
If an
.Pa nvmupdate.cfg
manifest is found in the current directory, or one is given with
.Fl c ,
each card is matched against it and reported as one of
.Cm CURRENT ,
.Cm UPGRADE ,
.Cm NO_MATCH ,
.Cm MISSING_BIN
or
.Cm ERROR ;
without a manifest the status is
.Cm NO_CFG .
For a card that can be upgraded, the image, the reset type it requires
.Pq Cm REBOOT No or Cm POWER
and the exact
.Cm image-apply
command line are printed.
.Pp
With
.Fl q ,
one line per card is printed in a stable, machine-readable format:
ports, NVM version, firmware version, EETRACK ID, status, image and
reset type.
.It Cm dump Op Fl o Ar file
Read the entire 64 KB NVM as raw bytes.
With
.Fl o Ar file ,
the contents are written to
.Ar file ;
otherwise to standard output.
Writing the binary blob to a terminal is refused; redirect or use
.Fl o .
.It Cm show
Read the NVM header and print a summary of the well-known module
pointers and the software checksum word.
.It Cm cfg-read Ar feature_id
Issue an NVM Config Read admin command (datasheet 3.4.10.4) for the
given 16-bit
.Ar feature_id
and print the 6-byte response.
For example,
.Ar feature_id
0xFFF1 reads the current link-mode selection (1x40G, 2x40G, or 4x10G;
see datasheet 3.4.5.7).
This command waits for the asynchronous completion event posted on
the chip's admin receive queue.
.It Cm selftest Op Fl y
Read NVM word 0x16 (the PBA Block pointer, an RW header word per
datasheet 3.4.5.3) and write the same value back via NVM_UPDATE with
the Last_Command bit set, exercising the asynchronous bank-swap and
the kernel's ARQ completion-event demultiplex.
The card performs a bank swap on Flash, but the NVM contents are
semantically unchanged (identical-value write keeps the software
checksum valid).
Read back and compare to confirm.
.Pp
Without
.Fl y ,
prints what would happen and exits.
Pass
.Fl y
to confirm and run the test.
.It Cm image-show Ar file
Parse an Intel NVM update image
.Pq Ar file
and print its NVM control word, software checksum, PBA number,
and the table of module pointers (with the update flow each module
needs).
This subcommand operates on a file and does not require
.Fl i .
.It Cm image-diff Ar file
Verify the chip against an Intel NVM image
.Pq Ar file ,
module by module.
For each NVM module the tool understands, the module is located via
the
.Em chip's
own header pointer
.Pq not the image's, since a per-module update relocates modules and swaps banks ,
its bytes are read from the chip, and they are compared to the image's
copy of that module.
Each module is reported as one of:
.Bl -tag -width PRESERVED -compact
.It Cm MATCH
chip content equals the image.
.It Cm DIFFER
chip content differs; for a module the image carries, this means the
module is not yet updated on the chip.
.It Cm MANAGED
a chip-populated register auto-load or data-recovery module
.Pq e.g. Cm GLOBR Auto-load , Cm SW-Data-Recovery :
it differs from the static image by design
.Pq the firmware fills it with computed/runtime state
and is excluded from the pass/fail tally.
.It Cm PRESERVED
a card-identity field
.Pq MAC, VPD, PBA, PXE, VLAN, boot config
that an apply deliberately leaves untouched.
.It Cm ABSENT No / Cm SKIP
the module is not present in the image, or its size cannot be
determined.
.El
.Pp
A correctly updated card reports every authenticated and
non-authenticated module as
.Cm MATCH
and zero
.Cm DIFFER ;
the
.Cm MANAGED
modules differ identically to how Intel's own tool leaves them.
This is the recommended way to confirm an
.Cm image-apply
took effect.
.It Xo
.Cm match
.Fl c Ar nvmupdate.cfg
.Op Fl e Ar eepid
.Xc
Parse Intel's
.Ar nvmupdate.cfg
manifest and identify which packaged
.Pa .bin
image is appropriate for a given EETRACK ID.
Without
.Fl e ,
.Fl i Ar ifname
must be given and the chip's current EETRACK ID is read from words
0x2d-0x2e of its NVM.
With
.Fl e ,
the EETRACK ID is taken from the command line as a hex string.
.Pp
The result names the matching
.Cm NVM IMAGE
file, its target EEPID, the required reset type
.Pq POWER No or Cm REBOOT ,
and whether the chip is already at the matched image or needs to
upgrade to it (via the cfg's
.Cm REPLACES
list).
.It Cm verify-checksum
Read the chip's shadow RAM and verify the software checksum at
word 0x3f against the value an independent computation derives
from the same bytes (datasheet 6.1.4.1).
A match confirms that the chip's NVM state is internally consistent
and that this tool's checksum algorithm agrees with the firmware's.
.It Xo
.Cm image-apply
.Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl y
.Xc
Apply an Intel NVM image to the chip by running the per-module update
flow for each module in turn, in three phases:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Phase 1: the non-authenticated outside-shadow modules
.Pq datasheet 3.4.5.4 :
.Cm EMP Global ,
.Cm Manageability ,
.Cm EMP Settings ,
.Cm PHY Config Scripts ,
.Cm Configuration Metadata ,
and
.Cm External 25G PHY .
.It
Phase 2 (only with
.Fl s ,
.Sy experimental ) :
the read/write shadow-RAM modules
.Pq datasheet 3.4.5.3 .
This is not part of the supported upgrade: the chip's EMP regenerates
most of these modules on its own, and direct shadow writes have proven
fragile and chip-state-dependent.
See
.Sx CAVEATS .
.It
Phase 2b: the NVM header version words
.Pq Cm DEV_STARTER_VERSION No word 0x18, Cm EETRACK ID No words 0x2d-0x2e
and the recomputed software checksum, so the chip reports the new
image version after the upgrade.
.It
Phase 3: the authenticated outside-shadow modules
.Pq datasheet 3.4.5.5 :
.Cm PCIe Analog ,
.Cm PHY Analog ,
.Cm Option ROM ,
and
.Cm EMP Image .
.El
.Pp
.Cm EMP Image
is applied last: after its
.Cm LAST_COMMAND
the chip stops servicing admin-queue commands until power-cycle/reboot,
so it must be the final step.
Each authenticated module's RSA-2048 signature is verified by the chip
on
.Cm LAST_COMMAND
and a mismatch causes the chip to reject that module
.Pq the previous one stays active .
Modules absent from the image
.Pq pointer 0xffff/0x0000
are skipped.
.Pp
Without
.Fl y ,
.Cm image-apply
is a dry run: validates the image checksum, EETRACK ID, and per-module
source offsets, and prints the per-module plan.
With
.Fl y ,
the chip is actually written.
A reboot is required afterwards.
.It Xo
.Cm module-update
.Fl m Ar module
.Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl y
.Xc
Update a single NVM module from an Intel NVM image, using the same
per-module update flow as
.Cm image-apply .
The module is selected by name with
.Fl m .
.Pp
Authenticated outside-shadow modules
.Pq datasheet 3.4.5.5 ,
whose RSA-2048 signature the chip verifies on
.Cm LAST_COMMAND
before switching to them; if verification fails, the update is
rejected and the old module stays active:
.Bl -tag -width "globr-autoload" -compact
.It Cm option-rom
Option ROM: PXE and UEFI network boot code (1160\~KB).
.It Cm emp-image
EMP Image: the firmware of the embedded management processor
(EMP) that runs the controller (1160\~KB).
After it is written the chip stops servicing admin-queue commands
until the system is rebooted; see
.Sx CAVEATS .
.It Cm pcie-analog
PCIe Analog: PCIe SerDes analog settings (8\~KB).
.It Cm phy-analog
PHY Analog: Ethernet PHY analog settings (8\~KB).
.El
.Pp
Non-authenticated outside-shadow modules
.Pq datasheet 3.4.5.4 ,
written without a signature check:
.Bl -tag -width "globr-autoload" -compact
.It Cm emp-global
EMP Global: global settings of the EMP firmware (8\~KB).
.It Cm manageability
Manageability: configuration of the sideband manageability
interfaces, such as NC-SI (8\~KB).
.It Cm emp-settings
EMP Settings: further EMP firmware settings (8\~KB).
.It Cm phy-scripts
PHY Config Scripts: link and PHY configuration scripts run by the
firmware (8\~KB).
.It Cm config-meta
Configuration Metadata: the description of the configurable NVM
options (128\~KB).
.It Cm ext-25g-phy
External 25G PHY: firmware for an external 25G PHY, present only on
cards that have one (128\~KB).
.El
.Pp
An outside-shadow module is written sector by sector with
interleaved
.Cm NVM_Erase
and
.Cm NVM_Update
admin-queue commands, the final command carrying
.Cm LAST_COMMAND .
.Pp
Shadow-RAM modules
.Pq datasheet 3.4.5.3
live inside the 64 KB shadow RAM and are written with
.Cm NVM_Update
followed by a recomputed software checksum.
Writing them is
.Sy experimental ;
see
.Sx CAVEATS .
The NVM header word holding each module's pointer is given in
parentheses:
.Bl -tag -width "globr-autoload" -compact
.It Cm auto-gen-ptrs
Auto Generated Pointers (0x07).
.It Cm pcir-autoload
Registers loaded on PCIe reset (0x08).
.It Cm por-autoload
Registers loaded on power-on reset (0x38).
.It Cm globr-autoload
Registers loaded on global reset (0x3b).
.It Cm corer-autoload
Registers loaded on core reset (0x3c).
.It Cm emp-sr
EMP shadow-RAM settings (0x48).
.It Cm feature-cfg
Feature Configuration (0x49).
.It Cm core-mem
Core memory configuration (0x4a).
.It Cm immediate
Immediate Fields (0x4e).
.El
.Pp
Card-identity data
.Pq PBA, boot configuration, SAN MAC, VPD, PXE and VLAN settings
is never written.
.Pp
Without
.Fl y ,
prints the plan and exits.
With
.Fl y ,
the chip is actually written.
A reboot is required afterwards.
.El
.Sh SECURITY
Reading the NVM
.Pq Cm dump , show , scan , image-diff , verify-checksum , No etc.
is permitted at any
.Xr securelevel 7 .
Writing the NVM
.Pq Cm image-apply , module-update No with Fl y
opens a write session, which the kernel allows only at securelevel 0
or below.
In the default multi-user securelevel
.Pq 1 ,
with the network up, opening a write session fails with
.Er EPERM .
This prevents a privileged remote attacker from flashing malicious
firmware on a running, network-reachable system: to update firmware,
boot into single-user mode
.Pq which is administered from the local console and starts no network services ,
apply the image, and reboot.
The complete procedure is shown in
.Sx EXAMPLES .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std ixlnvm
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following shows a complete NVM update of a system with two
X710-DA2 cards.
.Pp
First, list the cards and their current NVM and firmware versions:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ixlnvm
scanning ixl(4) interfaces; nvmupdate.cfg not found, inventory only

ixl0,ixl1  (pci 5:0, 2 ports)
           nvm 9.30  fw 9.130.73618  eetrack 0x8000e5d7  NO_CFG
ixl2,ixl3  (pci 7:0, 2 ports)
           nvm 9.20  fw 9.120.73026  eetrack 0x8000d87f  NO_CFG

summary: 0 upgrade, 0 current, 0 no-match, 0 missing-bin, 0 error
.Ed
.Pp
Download the
.Dq NVM Update Utility for Intel Ethernet Network Adapter 700 Series
from Intel.
This link always leads to the latest version:
.Pp
.Lk https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/download/18190/
.Pp
Intel requires accepting a license in a web browser before the
download starts, so the package cannot be fetched with
.Xr ftp 1 .
The download is a ZIP archive containing one package per operating
system.
.Nm
uses only the
.Pa nvmupdate.cfg
manifest and the
.Pa .bin
images from the Linux package, not Intel's update tool.
Extract the Linux package, for example with
.Ic unzip
from packages, and unpack it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# unzip 700Series_NVMUpdatePackage_v9_58.zip \e
    700Series_NVMUpdatePackage_v9_58_Linux.tar.gz
# tar -xzf 700Series_NVMUpdatePackage_v9_58_Linux.tar.gz
.Ed
.Pp
Run
.Nm
again from the directory containing the manifest
.Pq or pass it with Fl c
to see which cards can be upgraded, and the command to do so:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# cd 700Series/Linux_x64
# ixlnvm
scanning ixl(4) interfaces against nvmupdate.cfg (120 entries)

ixl0,ixl1  (pci 5:0, 2 ports)
           nvm 9.30  fw 9.130.73618  eetrack 0x8000e5d7  UPGRADE
           upgrade to X710DA2_9p58_CFGID7p2_OEMGEN.bin (reset REBOOT)
           apply:  ixlnvm -i ixl0 image-apply -f X710DA2_9p58_CFGID7p2_OEMGEN.bin -y
ixl2,ixl3  (pci 7:0, 2 ports)
           nvm 9.20  fw 9.120.73026  eetrack 0x8000d87f  UPGRADE
           upgrade to X710DA2_9p58_CFGID7p2_OEMGEN.bin (reset REBOOT)
           apply:  ixlnvm -i ixl2 image-apply -f X710DA2_9p58_CFGID7p2_OEMGEN.bin -y

summary: 2 upgrade, 0 current, 0 no-match, 0 missing-bin, 0 error
.Ed
.Pp
Writing the NVM requires
.Xr securelevel 7
0
.Pq see Sx SECURITY .
Reboot and, at the
.Xr boot 8
prompt, boot into single-user mode, which runs at securelevel 0:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
boot> boot -s
.Ed
.Pp
In single-user mode only the root file system is mounted, read-only.
Mount the other file systems so that
.Pa /usr/sbin/ixlnvm
and the unpacked package are available
.Pq run Xr fsck 8 No first if Xr mount 8 No refuses ,
and confirm the securelevel:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# mount -a
# sysctl kern.securelevel
kern.securelevel=0
.Ed
.Pp
Change to the package directory again and run the
.Dq apply:
line printed for each card.
Only one port per card needs to be given.
A complete run looks like this:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# cd 700Series/Linux_x64
# ixlnvm -i ixl2 image-apply -f X710DA2_9p58_CFGID7p2_OEMGEN.bin -y
image:      X710DA2_9p58_CFGID7p2_OEMGEN.bin
  size:       4194304 bytes
  firmware:   9.58
  EETRACK ID: 0x800104df
  checksum:   0xea10 [ok]
chip:       ixl2
  EETRACK ID: 0x8000d87f (different)

plan: per-module update across three phases

  Phase 1: FLOW_NONAUTH (datasheet 3.4.5.4)
    EMP Global            src 0x026000     2 sectors (update 0x09, erase 0x46)
    Manageability         src 0x028000     2 sectors (update 0x0e, erase 0x46)
    EMP Settings          src 0x02a000     2 sectors (update 0x0f, erase 0x46)
    PHY Config Scripts    src 0x024000     2 sectors (update 0x3d, erase 0x46)
    Configuration Meta    src 0x3b4000    32 sectors (update 0x4d, erase 0x44)
    External 25G PHY      (not in image, skipped)

  Phase 2: FLOW_SHADOW (datasheet 3.4.5.3, batched) [EXPERIMENTAL]
    DISABLED (default).  -s enables it, but the EMP
    regenerates these modules and direct writes are
    fragile; the supported flow does not use -s.

  Phase 2b: NVM header version words (always on)
    word 0x18 (DEV_STARTER_VERSION) <- image
    words 0x2d/0x2e (EETRACK ID)    <- image
    word 0x3f (software checksum)   <- recomputed [LAST]

  Phase 3: FLOW_AUTH (datasheet 3.4.5.5)
    PCIe Analog           src 0x020000     2 sectors (update 0x03, erase 0x46)
    PHY Analog            src 0x022000     2 sectors (update 0x04, erase 0x46)
    Option ROM            src 0x02e000   290 sectors (update 0x05, erase 0x40)
    EMP Image             src 0x150000   290 sectors (update 0x0b, erase 0x40)  <- EMP last

  FLOW_AUTH modules: chip verifies RSA-2048 signature on
    LAST_COMMAND; mismatch keeps the old module active.
  FLOW_NONAUTH/FLOW_SHADOW: written without signature check.
  reboot required after completion.

*** writing chip NVM.  DO NOT INTERRUPT.
  EMP Global: sector    2/2  write [LAST]
  Manageability: sector    2/2  write [LAST]
  EMP Settings: sector    2/2  write [LAST]
  PHY Config Scripts: sector    2/2  write [LAST]
  Configuration Meta: sector   32/32  write [LAST]
  reading shadow: 64 / 64 KB

  header: nvm version 0x9058 (word 0x18)
  header: EETRACK 0x800104df (words 0x2d/0x2e)
  header: checksum 0xbdce at word 0x3f [LAST]
  PCIe Analog: sector    2/2  write [LAST]
  PHY Analog: sector    2/2  write [LAST]
  Option ROM: sector  290/290  write [LAST]
  EMP Image: sector  290/290  write [LAST]

Image apply completed.  Reboot the system to load the new firmware.
# ixlnvm -i ixl0 image-apply -f X710DA2_9p58_CFGID7p2_OEMGEN.bin -y
\&...
.Ed
.Pp
Do not interrupt the update or remove power while it runs.
Leaving out
.Fl y
prints the plan without writing anything.
.Pp
Once all cards are written, reboot
.Pq or, for a card whose reset type is Cm POWER , power the system off and on again :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# reboot
.Ed
.Pp
After the reboot, running
.Nm
in the package directory reports each updated card as
.Cm CURRENT .
To compare a card with the image module by module:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ixlnvm -i ixl2 image-diff X710DA2_9p58_CFGID7p2_OEMGEN.bin
.Ed
.Pp
Dump the NVM of
.Li ixl0
to a file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ixlnvm -i ixl0 dump -o ixl0.nvm
.Ed
.Pp
Display the NVM header summary:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ixlnvm -i ixl0 show
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ixl 4 ,
.Xr ifconfig 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared in
.Ox 8.1 .
